7. Ethical use — the project's covenant
---------------------------------------

This project exists to defend open societies, scientific inquiry, and
the dignity of all life against organised coercion. With that intent
stated plainly, its contributors request that this work — its
synthesis, its tools, its forecasts, and anything derived from them —
never be used:

  (a) to develop, target, justify, or operate violence or coercion
      against any person or group;

  (b) to build instruments of mass surveillance, predictive policing,
      social scoring, watch-listing, or population control. The
      early-warning and forecasting methods discussed here are
      intended to flag *risk to populations and the conduct of power*
      so that protective and accountability action can be taken — NOT
      to monitor, score, profile, or pre-emptively act against
      individuals. Inverting an early-warning tool into a targeting
      tool is the precise abuse this covenant forbids;

  (c) to suppress scientific inquiry, free expression, or the press,
      or to persecute minorities, dissidents, migrants, or any
      vulnerable group;

  (d) to harm, endanger, or diminish any form of life, or to degrade
      the ecosystems life depends on;

  (e) to claim the project's endorsement of any such use.

This is a covenant, not a clause bolted onto the underlying licenses:
the copyleft licenses (CC BY-SA 4.0, GPL-3.0-or-later) do not allow
added restrictions, and the CC0 1.0 waiver gives up all rights. That
keeps the work freely reusable for open science, and it means the
project relies on the reader's good faith. A defense against
authoritarianism, built with tools that could serve authoritarianism,
would defeat its own purpose. If you proceed against this covenant,
you do so without the project's blessing — and, for the prose and the
code, the licenses still require you to credit it. Reflect on how that
credit will read.


